Seems, that we refer to `doc` variable only with 'sandbox' security level, so:
```ts
const root: HML = select('body');
if (securityLevel === 'sandbox') {
    const sandboxElement: HTML = select(`#i${id}`);
    const doc: Document = sandboxElement.node()?.contentDocument ?? document;
    root = select(doc.body as HTMLIFrameElement);
}
```
Unless we need to return `doc` along with `svg` as a function result

> Seems, that we refer to `doc` variable only with 'sandbox' security level, so:
> 
> ```ts
> const root: HML = select('body');
> if (securityLevel === 'sandbox') {
>     const sandboxElement: HTML = select(`#i${id}`);
>     const doc: Document = sandboxElement.node()?.contentDocument ?? document;
>     root = select(doc.body as HTMLIFrameElement);
> }
> ```
> 
> Unless we need to return `doc` along with `svg` as a function result

Make sense, thanks! I was using this block of code in pieRenderer and it currently uses the `doc` variable, that why I assigned it outside.

> And a separate comment for this. Is there need to return `root`, `doc` along with `svg` for other diagrams? Info diagram is our lab animal in many cases, but not fully representative.

And don't think we need to do that. While I was working on converting pie files, I saw it [uses the `doc`](https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid/blob/23b6d53f80fb803562f13f86039da31a1cf632dd/packages/mermaid/src/diagrams/pie/pieRenderer.ts#L36) and [change some attributes](https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid/blob/23b6d53f80fb803562f13f86039da31a1cf632dd/packages/mermaid/src/diagrams/pie/pieRenderer.ts#L44) on it, which it should do that on the `svg` instead.
Some diagrams do that and I'll fix them when standardizing their files.

After more debugging, when I added the following line of code in [`/packages/diagrams/info/infoRenderer.ts`](https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid/blob/3d117815425cbd05105ec0f0192adc0be91931f2/packages/mermaid/src/diagrams/info/infoRenderer.ts#L21-L24):

```diff
if (securityLevel === 'sandbox') {
  const sandboxElement: HTML = select('#i' + id);
  document = sandboxElement.node()?.contentDocument;
+ log.info(document?.body)
}
```

It shows that the body is being use in the first time is the parent one, not sub body of `iframe` element:

